{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve for \\(\\prod_{n=1}^{100} \\psi(3^n)\\), we start by analyzing the given functional equation \\(\\psi(3x) + 3 \\psi(x) = \\psi^3(x)\\).\n\nFirst, let's denote \\(y = \\psi(x)\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[ \\psi(3x) + 3y = y^3. \\]\nRearranging this, we get:\n\\[ \\psi(3x) = y^3 - 3y. \\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the value of \\(\\psi(x)\\) for specific values of \\(x\\). We know that \\(\\psi(1) = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}\\). Let's denote this value by \\(a\\), so \\(a = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}\\).\n\nWe will check if \\(a\\) satisfies the functional equation. First, we need to find \\(\\psi(3)\\):\n\\[ \\psi(3) = a^3 - 3a. \\]\n\nNow, we need to check if \\(a^3 - 3a = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}}}\\). Let's denote \\(b = a^3 - 3a\\). We need to show that \\(b = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}}}\\).\n\nTo do this, we will assume that \\(\\psi(x) = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\cdots}}}\\) with \\(n\\) nested square roots. Let's denote this value by \\(c_n\\), where \\(c_1 = \\sqrt{2}\\), \\(c_2 = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}\\), \\(c_3 = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}\\), and so on. We have \\(a = c_3\\).\n\n","prefix_end":1117,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,369,1124,11520,19748,15159,77,28,16,92,47822,16,15,15,92,1124,30765,7,18,86167,10699,701,582,1191,553,41018,279,2661,15629,23606,1124,11520,30765,7,18,87,8,488,220,18,1124,30765,2075,8,284,1124,30765,61,18,2075,10699,3593,5338,11,1077,594,78064,17767,88,284,1124,30765,2075,10699,568,5005,279,23606,9044,510,78045,1124,30765,7,18,87,8,488,220,18,88,284,379,61,18,13,1124,921,49,682,81,8595,419,11,582,633,510,78045,1124,30765,7,18,87,8,284,379,61,18,481,220,18,88,13,1124,2533,5847,11,582,1184,311,8253,279,897,315,1124,11520,30765,2075,10699,8,369,3151,2750,3